==English==

===Etymology===
{{coin|en|Q16202556|in=1979|nobycat=1}},  in the ''{{w|Esquire (magazine)|Esquire}}'' article “America’s Most Powerful Lunch.”<ref>{{cite-web |title=A Wordsmith at Heart|work=Madison Magazine |url=http://www.madisonmagazine.com/article.php?section_id=918&xstate=view_story&story_id=211466 |accessdate=6 March 2009 |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090715061714/http://www.madisonmagazine.com/article.php?section_id=918&xstate=view_story&story_id=211466 |archivedate=15 July 2009 }}</ref><ref>Lee Eisenberg, “America’s Most Powerful Lunch.”, ''[[w:Esquire (magazine)|Esquire]],'' October 1979; Volume 92, No. 4 ([https://classic.esquire.com/article/1979/10/1/americas-most-powerful-lunch cover])</ref><ref>Article begins: “It begins in New York every working day at 12:30 P.M. in the Bar Room of The Four Seasons. It ends with the decisions and the deals that will keep you in books, clothes, wine, and ideas for the rest of the year. Look inside and see who’s eating there and who gets the best table. Unless you're very persistent – or lucky – this is as close as you’ll get.”</ref><ref>[http://www.barrypopik.com/index.php/new_york_city/entry/power_breakfast_power_lunch/ The Big Apple: Power Breakfast; Power Lunch], September 18, 2004; many references.</ref>

